"""
strongswan-dialer: bring up a VoWiFi (SWu) tunnel to the carrier's ePDG with a
private charon instance driven over vici, and expose it as a network interface
inside the `ims` netns.

Nothing is read from /etc/strongswan*: the dialer writes a throwaway
strongswan.conf under /run/nekoims, starts its own charon with
STRONGSWAN_CONF pointing at it, loads the connection over vici and removes
everything again on exit.

The tunnel is an XFRM interface (if_id), which is how stock strongSwan exposes
a route-based tunnel as a netdev; kernel-libipsec (a real TUN) isn't built
by most distros. The interface is moved into the netns, where the dialer
assigns the virtual IPs and default routes. charon itself stays in the init
netns, so the IKE/ESP traffic uses the normal uplink.

    sudo python3 simcard-server/server.py &
    sudo python3 dialers/strongswan-dialer.py [--epdg host] [--mnc-len 3]
    sudo ip netns exec ims ./build/nekoims

While the tunnel is up the P-CSCFs the ePDG assigned are in
/run/nekoims/pcscf (one per line), which nekoims reads when neither -p nor
"pcscf" in its config is given.

EAP-AKA needs RES/CK/IK from the USIM. Stock strongSwan has no client-side
backend that can reach a real card (eap-radius is EAP_SERVER only, the
3gpp2 card needs K), so this needs our strongSwan fork (branch `nekoims`):
its eap-aka-http plugin asks simcard-server, and it also carries the
Osmocom eap-sim-pcsc AKA patches (see AKA_BACKENDS). The fork's p-cscf
plugin is used when present to learn the P-CSCF from the ePDG.

Requires root, iproute2, a charon binary and the strongSwan python `vici`
package.

On Windows there are no network namespaces or XFRM interfaces. charon-svc
(the fork's Windows build, see --remote) runs kernel-libipsec: ESP is done in
userspace and the tunnel is a Wintun adapter (--ifname, "NekoIMS"), which
gets the virtual IPs, a host route to each P-CSCF and a default route with a
high metric. Windows' strong host model sends whatever is bound to the
tunnel address out of the tunnel, and the rest of the host keeps its own
routes. IKE runs from random local ports since Windows' IKEEXT service holds
500/4500, and ESP is always UDP-encapsulated. vici and the SIM server are on
loopback TCP (127.0.0.1:4502 and :8888). Run it as Administrator:

    python simcard-server/server.py
    python dialers/strongswan-dialer.py --remote <windows zip URL>
    build/nekoims.exe     (reads the P-CSCF from %ProgramData%\\NekoIMS)
"""
